.TH NISEMONO 1 "2026-11-10" "nisemono" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nisemono \- find out whether a card or USB drive holds what it claims
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nisemono
.br
.B nisemono probe
.RI [ options ] " device" | mount_point
.br
.B nisemono fill
.I mount_point
.br
.B nisemono fix
.IR device | mount_point
.br
.B nisemono doctor
.br
.B nisemono version
.br
.B nisemono help
.SH DESCRIPTION
A counterfeit SD card or USB drive reports more space than its flash holds.
Writes past the real flash land on an earlier address or are lost, so files
copied to it look fine until they are read back.
.PP
.B nisemono probe
writes a tagged block on an even grid across the whole size the disk reports,
and only after every block is written reads them all back. A block that comes
back with another block's tag, or with no tag at all, marks the end of the
real flash. probe then tests single blocks to find the exact point where the
real flash ends: it tries the divisors of the distance between two blocks that
came back from one place, then halves the range between the last good and the
first bad block. The tags carry a random code for each run, so data left by an
earlier run is never mistaken for this one.
.PP
A fake verdict is proof. A pass means every tested block came back, which
catches the fakes sold today, but a card that wraps at an address no test
block lands on can still pass. A larger
.B \-\-samples
narrows that gap.
.PP
A disk is named by its device, such as
.IR /dev/disk4 ,
or by any volume mounted from it, such as
.IR /Volumes/SD ,
which stands for the whole disk.
.PP
Before anything is written, nisemono prints what it is about to test and asks
for the device name to be typed back. The probe overwrites the blocks it
tests, and on a fake card it overwrites more, so copy anything you need first.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B probe
Test the disk and report its real size.
.TP
.B fill
Fill the free space of a mounted volume with check files whose bytes encode
their place, read every byte back past the cache, and remove them, also when
interrupted. Files already on the volume are never touched. Slow, and
destroys nothing.
.TP
.B fix
Probe the disk and, when it is fake, write a new partition table with one FAT
partition named NISEMONO that ends inside the real size. Everything on the
disk is lost, after its name is typed back. Needs
.B mformat
from mtools.
.TP
.B doctor
List the tools nisemono needs and whether each is installed. Exits non-zero only
when a required tool is missing.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and the platform.
.TP
.B help
Print the usage page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-samples " N"
Test at least
.I N
blocks across the disk. Default 256. More blocks find a real size that is not a
power of two more closely and take longer.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-internal
Allow an internal disk. The disk holding the running system is always refused.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as one JSON object on stdout, for scripts: the disk, the size
it reports, its real size, the size up to the last block that read back,
the verdict
.B pass
or
.BR fake ,
and the counts of grid blocks that read back, came back from a lower address
and were lost.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " name"
Colours of the terminal interface:
.BR kodougu ,
.BR catppuccin\-mocha ,
.BR catppuccin\-latte ,
.BR dracula ,
.BR nord ,
.BR gruvbox ,
.B tokyo\-night
or
.BR mono .
The default is
.BR kodougu .
.PP
Every option that takes a value also takes the form
.IR \-\-name = value ,
and
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NISEMONO_SAMPLES
Same as
.BR \-\-samples .
.TP
.B NISEMONO_ALLOW_INTERNAL
Set to
.B y
for the same effect as
.BR \-\-allow\-internal .
.TP
.B NISEMONO_JSON
Set to
.B y
for the same effect as
.BR \-\-json .
.PP
An option on the command line wins over the environment.
.TP
.B NISEMONO_THEME
The theme when
.B \-\-theme
is not given.
.TP
.B KODOUGU_THEME
The theme of every kodougu tool, when neither
.B \-\-theme
nor
.B NISEMONO_THEME
is set.
.TP
.B KODOUGU_ACCENT
A colour such as #CBA6F7 that replaces the accent of the theme.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Turns colour off in every theme.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every test block came back, 1 when the disk is fake, on any failure and
on any refusal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kiyome (1),
.BR kakikomi (1),
.BR dd (1)
